  "schema": "component-exact-evidence-preservation-v1",
  "scope_limit": "For an integer matrix with two columns and a finite nonempty row set, the theorem uses the actual real column span and the actual line generated by a primitive signed integer parameter pair. The primitive premise is `Int.gcd x y = 1` on the parameters. For any real upper bound `C` on every squared row norm, `plane_value_le` bounds the plane value by the line value plus `1/2 * sqrt(C / (x^2+y^2))`.\n\nIf the actual plane value is at least `2/11` and the actual line value is strictly below `1/6`, the proof gives `x^2+y^2 < 1089*C`. Taking the exact finite maximum of the squared row norms gives the natural-number bound `x.natAbs^2 + y.natAbs^2 < 1089 * maxRowSq A`. An explicit coordinate-equality theorem carries this bound to a natural six-speed vector. It does not separately require the natural speed vector to be positive, distinct or primitive, and adds no rank, integer-left-inverse, four-speed or six-speed-classification premise.\n\nThe plane gap remains an assumption. This theorem does not classify the 78 maps or complete the six-speed spectrum.",
  "source_commit": "6932e0ba9c532f86fbf9e5fb23aff9e55b8e99f9"
}
